To correctly chain multiple else if statements in C++, ensure each else if follows an if or else without any syntax errors. Here’s how you can structure your code:

```cpp
int main() {
    if (a >= b && a >= c) {
        // do something
    } else if (a >= d && a >= e) {
        // do something else
    } else if (a >= f && a >= g) {
        // else if can follow an else in the chain
    }
}
```

**Chaining Else If:**
1. After an `if`, you can directly add an `else if` for the next condition.
2. After an `else`, you can also add an `else if` for the next condition without introducing a new else.

**Correct Structure:**  
```cpp
if (condition1) {
    // statements
} else if (condition2) {
    // statements
} else if (condition3) {
    // statements
}
```

